        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nlmd.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Winnebago and Potawatomi Indians were the first people to resort to the shores of North Lake before pioneers arrived around 1837.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nlmd.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Henry Shears helped shape the early development of North Lake by building a sawmill and a gristmill between 1850 and 1853.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Lake%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "North Lake spans 440 acres and reaches a maximum depth of over 78 feet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Lake%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Four different inlets feed North Lake, including the Big and Little Oconomowoc Rivers, Mason Creek, and Cornell Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
